WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.359
LINDSAY: Hey Princess!

00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:04.750
Yeah, yeah, for the next It’s Lit episode--what
do you want to do for the next topic?

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.850
PRINCESS: Something snappy!

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.450
We’re thinking fun!&nbsp;

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.099
Lindsay: something SHORT

00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:11.820
Princess: You know, something quick and digestible.&nbsp;

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.580
LINDSAY: It’s not like this script needs
to be War &amp; Peace.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.440
LINDSAY: So it’s finally come to this.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.720
It’s finally time we talk about it.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:21.900
The big book.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.180
The long boy.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.490
That novel you negatively compare everything
and anything to when things start to get long

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:28.670
and unwieldy.&nbsp;

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:35.140
PRINCESS: That Ur example of books that are
almost too long to be allowed, that only nerds

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.780
and pedants read so they can say they have.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:46.040
The GOAT of literary humble bragging, in whom’s
shadow Joyce’s Ulysses and Pynchon’s Gravity’s

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.230
Rainbow quake.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.980
BOTH: War and Peace.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.020
LINDSAY: So you’ve read it… right?

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.240
PRINCESS: I thought you had.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:04.570
According to Tolstoy himself, War and Peace
was "not a novel, even less is it a poem,

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.760
and still less a historical chronicle."

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.400
And in this day and age of publishing, where
word count, “readability”, and topical

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:18.890
relevance are the lifeline of getting a novel
to print, we look at books like War &amp; Peace

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.740
as something of a relic.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.890
Whereas most publishers today would advise
that general adult fiction hover around 120k

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:36.450
words, Tolstoy was perfectly comfortable clocking
in at a cool 587k words with War &amp; Peace.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.150
Which, while we’re here, let’s dispel
a couple of notions real quick about it being

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.580
the longest book.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:55.940
Other popular books of similar or greater
length include: Lord of the Rings, Les Miserables,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.189
Atlas Shrugged

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:06.650
But these other books, despite their length,
have embedded themselves in the public consciousness

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:12.920
more than War &amp; Peace-- And yet W&amp;P floats
on our periphery as mostly being code for

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.370
“long” and “Russian”.&nbsp;

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:16.370
So what gives?

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.420
So how does one even begin to describe War
and Peace?

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.800
In the words of Northwestern University professor
and literary critic, Gary Saul Morson.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:31.400
“With its endless plot lines that go nowhere,
its innumerable characters who appear only

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:40.140
to disappear, its endless mass of disconnected
and contingent events, the book seems a fabric

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.870
of lost threads, a work without structure
and backbone, it [...] ought to be a failure.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.740
And yet it is plainly the greatest novel ever
written.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.069
Well, that … simplified things???&nbsp;

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:58.959
It’s hard to condense War &amp; Peace’s plot
down to something easily digestible--this

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:05.500
is why every adaptation of it tends to be
300 hours long like the BBC adaptations, or

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:13.690
focused on only short sections, like the musical
Natasha, Pierre and the Great Comet of 1812,

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:15.050
or just….

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:20.690
Bad like the Audrey Hepburn version from 1956.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:28.430
But at its heart, War and Peace concerns itself
with the lives of three young nobles throughout

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:34.629
the beginning, height, and post-War years
of Russia’s foray in the Napoleonic Wars.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:43.980
There’s Pierre, a nerd of inhuman strength
who comes into wealth and nobility by circumstance,

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:51.650
and angsts about how to use these resources
ethically and find personal happiness.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:04:00.819
There’s Natasha, the saucy ingenue who feels
pressured to marry well because of her family’s

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:06.129
financial carelessness and her disquiet at
not being able to follow her heart.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:13.680
And then there’s Andrei - the jock prince
from old money, who finds his life so deeply

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.159
unfulfilling to the point of nihilism where
he’s like “Die in war for glory?

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:24.349
Sounds cool, pencil me in.”

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:29.469
while the book follows their lives over the
span of fifteen years, Tolstoy also manages

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:34.860
to weave in and out of the lives of hundreds
of other characters--including real historical

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.860
figures, like Napoleon Bonaparte.&nbsp;

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.360
&nbsp;&nbsp;
even from its first publication, readers were

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:46.930
hard pressed to be able to classify it, with
its operatic scale, and large, tangential

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.259
essays inserted into the narrative… you
know, for flavor.&nbsp;

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:57.400
So what about this makes War and Peace inherently
memorable, besides being something literary

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.659
size-queens could rejoice over?

00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:07.330
War and Peace was first published as a magazine
serial between 1865 and 1867 under the title

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:15.339
The Year 1805, and subsequently published
in its entirety as War and Peace in 1869 (nice).

00:05:15.339 --> 00:05:20.900
Tolstoy originally intended it to be about
the life of a Decembrist aristocrat returning

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.020
from exile in Siberia in the 1850s.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:34.210
But then he decided he couldn’t just write
about that, so he ALSO needed to give context

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.559
and write about--

00:05:35.559 --> 00:05:43.960
The Decembrist Revolt of 1825, when a section
of aristocrats/military officers politely

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:48.080
rebelled against the new Czar of Russia, Nicholas
I.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.270
But Tolstoy, who is a lore fanboy, decides
one also can’t talk about the Decembrist

00:05:54.270 --> 00:06:03.349
Revolution without talking about the War of
1812 and the Napoleonic invasion of Russia.

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:10.089
So he writes himself into a 600k word corner
about that instead.

00:06:10.089 --> 00:06:12.879
Relatable author content.

00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:19.900
But more importantly, fiction writing du jour
was in a strange spot in the 1860s.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:27.229
Popular fiction wavered between quickly-published,
topical stories that Tolstoy despised--thinking

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:33.839
of them as literary clickbait that did nothing
to advance the craft--and a love of longer

00:06:33.839 --> 00:06:39.449
historical fiction like Sir Walter Scott’s
Ivanhoe, which Tolstoy found overly romantic

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:47.229
and solipsistic--only celebrating “important
men” when he believed that the day-to-day

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:52.479
lives of average people were what really mattered.

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:57.259
Tolstoy’s&nbsp; frustration with writing that
pertained to real life and history could be

00:06:57.259 --> 00:07:01.229
best summed up in hi own words:

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:07.779
When an officer fell on the field of Borodino
[...] do not imagine that he rejoiced about

00:07:07.779 --> 00:07:13.059
saving his fatherland [...]. On the contrary
he thought about his mother, the woman he

00:07:13.059 --> 00:07:19.709
had loved, the joys and smallness of life
[...]. And Napoleon, Kutuzov, the Grand Army...

00:07:19.709 --> 00:07:23.679
all seemed to him pitiful and insignificant
by comparison.

00:07:23.679 --> 00:07:31.879
With that in mind, War &amp; Peace becomes a Las
Vegas buffet of questions regarding ethics:

00:07:31.879 --> 00:07:36.580
What does it mean to love one’s country
when its actions wreak violence and sow discord?

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:42.159
What obligations do we have to our fellow
humans when wealth has placed us in a position

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:43.240
of comfort?

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:48.369
Is glory of the self greater than love of
another?

00:07:48.369 --> 00:07:54.449
And should you really, truly be allowed to
wrestle a bear after drinking enough vodka

00:07:54.449 --> 00:07:56.120
to kill a horse?

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:59.550
(Yes, this also happens.)

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:04.879
Even though it was immediately popular upon
publication, War &amp; Peace was criticized on

00:08:04.879 --> 00:08:11.550
both sides of the political aisle--it focused
too much on the nobility and Russian’s serfdom

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:17.740
and imperial past to progressives, and to
conservatives it dragged notable figures of

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.169
the country’s past through the mud and was
generally “unpatriotic.”

00:08:22.169 --> 00:08:29.559
And perhaps that is War and Peace’s most
enduring quality---that, while Tolstoy understood

00:08:29.559 --> 00:08:36.719
capturing objective truth was impossible,
it was important to look at the interior lives

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:40.520
of the common Man to get to the heart of truth.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:45.560
That literature is not just about heroes and
villains, but, as writer Henry James once

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.720
described War &amp; Peace, the “wonderful mass
of life.”&nbsp;

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:54.371
There’s a relative lack of English-language
scholarship devoted specifically to War &amp; Peace,

00:08:54.371 --> 00:08:55.371
especially in recent years, and one has to
wonder how much of this is because of the

00:08:55.371 --> 00:08:56.371
book’s sheer scale and intimidating reputation
and how much of it is because it was Tolstoy’s

00:08:56.371 --> 00:08:57.371
intention to not make it easy to dissect.

00:08:57.371 --> 00:09:01.540
In his essay for the Cambridge Companion to
Tolstoy, Gary Saul Morson had this to say:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:07.670
Tolstoy explains that he has deliberately
avoided structure and closure, because those

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:11.610
usual literary devices violate presentness.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:18.060
Where there is structure, there is no contingency,
and each present moment is already weighted

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.089
by the advance plan into which it must fit.

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:28.410
But life is not like that; it is rife with
contingency and loose ends are never all resolved.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:34.930
An author who truly aspires to realism must
find an alternative to structure…

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:42.220
Life for Tolstoy was not characterized by
the binaries of good or bad, hero or villain,

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:49.019
great or small, comedic or tragic--but by
where people’s actions fell on the constantly

00:09:49.019 --> 00:09:51.730
changing gradient between.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:58.690
He understood that characters feel more real
to readers in their small triumphs--comforting

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:03.379
a friend, picking away at a balalaika--and
failures.

00:10:03.379 --> 00:10:07.329
He understood that nuance is a feature, not
a bug.

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:08.899
He clearly had never been on Twitter.

00:10:08.899 --> 00:10:13.740
LINDSAY:&nbsp; War &amp; Peace’s legacy to the literary
world is not so much its length but it’s

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:18.440
contribution to Realism and the the idea that
everyone--from kings to sled drivers--has

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.540
a vast interiority within them.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:25.139
PRINCESS: Pair this with Tolstoy’s own words
on art--“If art is an important matter,

00:10:25.139 --> 00:10:31.730
a spiritual blessing, essential for all men
[...] then it should be accessible to everyone.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:38.279
And if, as in our day, it is not accessible
to all men, then one of two things: either

00:10:38.279 --> 00:10:44.180
art is not the vital matter it is represented
to be or that art which we call art is not

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.180
the real thing.”

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.269
LINDSAY: Though we take it for granted in
general fiction today, Tolstoy helped to up-end

00:10:49.269 --> 00:10:53.920
the idea that literature should only concern
itself and be written for the feelings of

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.430
larger-than-life characters and heroes.&nbsp;

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:00.939
Midway through the novel, after our heroine
Natasha has disgraced herself and been shunned

00:11:00.939 --> 00:11:05.330
by Moscow society, Pierre goes to visit her
to rebuke her as everyone else has.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:10.139
But when he sees her complete despair, he
is inevitably compelled to comfort Natasha

00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:13.509
as a friend would and quietly realize his
love for her.

00:11:13.509 --> 00:11:18.259
Tolstoy describes the small and intimate revelation
as Pierre leaves her house as follows:

00:11:18.259 --> 00:11:22.300
“An immense expanse of dark starry sky presented
itself to Pierre’s eyes.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.639
[...] Surrounded and sprinkled on all sides
by stars [...] shone the enormous and brilliant

00:11:26.639 --> 00:11:31.410
comet of 1812…. which was said to portend
all kinds of woes and the end of the world.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:34.839
In Pierre, however, that comet … aroused
no feeling of fear.

00:11:34.839 --> 00:11:40.220
On the contrary he gazed joyfully, his eyes
moist with tears, at this bright comet which,

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:44.899
having traveled in its orbit with inconceivable
velocity through immeasurable space, seemed

00:11:44.899 --> 00:11:49.779
suddenly—like an arrow piercing the earth—to
remain fixed in a chosen spot… shining and

00:11:49.779 --> 00:11:53.360
displaying its white light amid countless
other scintillating stars.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.670
It seemed to Pierre that this comet fully
responded to what was passing in his own softened

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.230
and uplifted soul, now blossoming into a new
life.”

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:08.930
PRINCESS: In War and Peace, Tolstoy gave us
a defining viewpoint of hope: that in a weary

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:16.980
world of global upheaval, war, death, and
political machination, the goodness of everyday

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:23.170
people all feels minute and fleeting--but
that the goodness is what survives.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:32.249
(But please don’t wrestle bears, we beg
you)

